# 11 — Coroutines: `spawn` a function, and watch it through a channel.
#
# `spawn f(x)` runs `f` as a coroutine and returns immediately. Nothing comes
# back — there is no handle to hold and nothing to join — so a channel is the
# only way to see what the coroutine did. `chan[T]()` makes one, `ch <- v`
# sends into it, and `<-ch` receives.
#
# A receive answers `Result[int]`: either a value, or the fact that the channel
# has closed. The end of a stream is therefore something you read, not a crash.
# `!` takes the value and aborts if there is none; `if v := <-ch` takes it only
# when one is there.
#
# Nothing below says when the channel ends, because nothing has to: a channel
# closes by itself once its last sender lets go of it. A channel type names
# which way an end points — `chan[int]<-` sends only, `<-chan[int]` receives
# only — and those two are what make "its last sender" exact.
#
# beyond-the-seed: a directional channel end. The bootstrap seed refuses one by
# name rather than approximate the per-direction refcount that keeps auto-close
# exact, so this example is built by `zerg` — which is what `make examples` uses.
fn squares(out: chan[int]<-, n: int) {
	mut i := 1

	for i <= n {
		out <- i * i # unbuffered, so each send waits for its receive
		i = i + 1
	}
}

# A source is a factory: it makes the channel, starts the producer on it, and
# hands back the receiving end alone. Two things then hold: `ch` belongs to
# this scope and is given back when the scope ends, so `source` stops being a
# sender the moment it returns; and the caller is handed `<-chan[int]`, which
# cannot send, so the caller can never hold the channel open by accident. That
# leaves `squares` as the only sender — and the channel closes when it returns.
#
# (A producer that wants to end its stream early, before it runs out of values,
# says `close(out)`. This one has nothing to cut short.)
fn source(n: int) -> <-chan[int] {
	ch := chan[int]()
	spawn squares(ch, n)

	return ch # a two-way end narrows to receive-only on the way out
}

fn main() {
	rx := source(4)

	print <-rx! # 1 — one receive, one value

	# `for v in rx` is that receive repeated: the channel IS the iterator, and
	# it yields until the close ends the loop. This is why the language needs
	# no `yield` and no generator type — the coroutine above is the generator,
	# and its send is the yield.
	for v in rx {
		print v # 4, then 9, then 16
	}

	# one producer on one channel means one order, so the five lines this
	# program prints are the same on every run and under any number of worker
	# threads. Past the close a receive still answers, and here it is the
	# `else` that runs.
	if v := <-rx {
		print v
	} else {
		print "the channel is closed"
	}
}
